    "answer_baseline": "Based on the provided context, the following courts can issue a writ of habeas corpus:\n\n1. **The Supreme Court**: \n   * It has the power to issue writs in the nature of habeas corpus for the enforcement of any of the rights conferred by Part III (Article 32(2)). \n   * Parliament may also by law confer power on the Supreme Court to issue writs of habeas corpus for purposes other than those mentioned in Article 32(2) (Article 139).\n\n2. **High Courts**: \n   * Every High Court has the power to issue writs in the nature of habeas corpus for the enforcement of rights conferred by Part III and for any other purpose within its territorial jurisdiction (Article 226(1)).\n\n3. **Any other court**: \n   * Parliament may by law empower any other court to exercise within the local limits of its jurisdiction the power to issue such writs (Article 32(3)).",
    "answer_reranked": "Based on the provided context, the following courts can issue a writ of habeas corpus:\n\n* **The Supreme Court:** Has the power to issue a writ of habeas corpus for the enforcement of any of the rights conferred by Part III (Article 32(2)). Additionally, Parliament may by law confer power on the Supreme Court to issue writs of habeas corpus for purposes other than those mentioned in Article 32(2) (Article 139).\n* **High Courts:** Every High Court has the power to issue a writ of habeas corpus for the enforcement of any of the rights conferred by Part III and for any other purpose (Article 226(1)).\n* **Other Courts:** Parliament may by law empower any other court to exercise within the local limits of its jurisdiction all or any of the powers exercisable by the Supreme Court (which includes issuing writs of habeas corpus) under Article 32(2) (Article 32(3))."
